title: Sending a signal one way or the other
mode: draw
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
parts: and or not
---- intro
A multiplexer picks one of many inputs. A **demultiplexer** does the opposite: it takes one input `d` and sends it to one of two outputs, chosen by `s`. The other output stays 0.

| s | y0 | y1 |
|---|----|----|
| 0 | `d` | 0 |
| 1 | 0 | `d` |

Each output is a signal let through, as two lessons ago, with a different enable.
---- task
Send `d` to `y0` when `s` is 0, and to `y1` when `s` is 1.
---- start
input d 0 0
input s 0 100
led y0 400 0
led y1 400 100
---- reference
input d, s
led y0, y1
y0 = d & ~s
y1 = d & s
---- answer
input d 0 0
input s 0 100
not n1 120 100
and g0 260 0
and g1 260 100
led y0 400 0
led y1 400 100
s -> n1.in
d -> g0.0
n1 -> g0.1
d -> g1.0
s -> g1.1
g0 -> y0
g1 -> y1
---- hint1
Two AND gates, both fed by `d`. One should let it through when `s` is 0, the other when `s` is 1.
---- hint2
`y1` is `d` AND `s`. `y0` is `d` AND NOT `s`.
---- solution
An AND of `d` and `s` drives `y1`; an AND of `d` and NOT `s` drives `y0`. Only one of them can let `d` through at a time.
---- done
Multiplexers gather signals in, demultiplexers send them out. Memory chips use both to read and write one cell out of millions.
